Ionic strength sets a screening scale under declared assumptions (@cm-electrolyte: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../sources/Physical-Chemistry-of-Interfaces-and-Colloids-Johannsmann.lingua#r_5FAT1MRFSVXVDS9Z6VCF4YM7TD", #clean-manufacturing "A-colloid-interaction-barrier-is-a-model-of-two-surfaces.lingua#r_5DXT48TR4E9RXFJPQJKG34ZJXX") { r_75M6D9MTPDEJQAMNP4DT5HM9T0
**Read:** [Physical chemistry of interfaces and colloids — Diethelm Johannsmann](../sources/Physical-Chemistry-of-Interfaces-and-Colloids-Johannsmann.lingua#r_5FAT1MRFSVXVDS9Z6VCF4YM7TD), section 10.5, deriving the equation through page 68.

For a dilute equilibrium electrolyte with constant permittivity, Poisson's equation and ideal Boltzmann ion distributions give a mean-field potential model. Linearizing at |zi eψ| much smaller than kBT gives ∇²ψ=κ²ψ, with κ²=e²Σzi²ni/(ε kBT). The bulk is electroneutral and ni are number densities. At a planar boundary, the decaying solution is ψ(z)=ψ0 exp(−κz).

The Debye length is λD=1/κ=√[ε RT/(2F²I)], where I=½Σzi²ci and ci uses mol/m³. For hypothetical water-like εr=78.4 at 298.15 K, a 1 mmol/L monovalent electrolyte gives approximately 9.61 nm. At equal salt concentration, a fully dissociated divalent/monovalent salt has three times the ionic strength and λD smaller by √3.

**Work:** Derive the linearization and check the conversion from mol/L. Test the exponential against its differential equation and charge balance. Identify where large potentials, finite ion size, ion correlations, specific adsorption or a changing dielectric environment defeat this model. A sub-nanometer calculated length is not evidence that a continuum model remains reliable there.

